„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙRAnd he continued there a year and six months, teaching the word of God among them.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses–„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙˆAnd when Gallio was the deputy of Achaia, the Jews made insurrection with one accord against Paul, and brought him to the judgment seat,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFsaying, This fellow persuadeth men to worship God contrary to the law.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¯And when Paul was now about to open his mouth, Gallio said unto the Jews, If it were a matter of wrong or wicked lewdness, O ye Jews, reason would that I should bear with you:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙsbut if it be a question of words and names, and of your law, look ye to it; for I will be no judge of such matters.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ)And he drave them from the judgment seat.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ—Then all the Greeks took Sosthenes, the chief ruler of the synagogue, and beat him before the judgment seat. And Gallio cared for none of those things.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses–„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÒAnd Paul after this tarried there yet a good while, and then took his leave of the brethren, and sailed thence into Syria, and with him Priscilla and Aquila; having shorn his head in Cenchrea: for he had a vow.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙsAnd he came to Ephesus, and left them there: but he himself entered into the synagogue, and reasoned with the Jews.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙGWhen they desired him to tarry longer time with them, he consented not;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¨but bade them farewell, saying, I must by all means keep this feast that cometh in Jerusalem: but I will return again unto you, if God will. And he sailed from Ephesus.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙaAnd when he had landed at CÃ¦sarea, and gone up, and saluted the church, he went down to Antioch.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ”And after he had spent some time there, he departed, and went over all the country of Galatia and Phrygia in order, strengthening all the disciples.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book+§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙtAnd a certain Jew named Apollos, born at Alexandria, an eloquent man, and mighty in the scriptures, came to Ephesus.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ©This man was instructed in the way of the Lord; and being fervent in the spirit, he spake and taught diligently the things of the Lord, knowing only the baptism of John.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ§And he began to speak boldly in the synagogue: whom when Aquila and Priscilla had heard, they took him unto them, and expounded unto him the way of God more perfectly.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ³And when he was disposed to pass into Achaia, the brethren wrote, exhorting the disciples to receive him: who, when he was come, helped them much which had believed through grace:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙhfor he mightily convinced the Jews, and that publickly, shewing by the scriptures that Jesus was Christ.¦layout ªlast_verse